{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you, speak to the children of Israel, saying: Nevertheless, you shall keep My sabbaths.</b> He added here the word 'and you,' because the day of the sabbath is fit to be attributed in its essence and origin to Moses, for he chose it in Egypt on the day of the sabbath, as is found in the Midrash (Exodus Rabbah 1:28), and in the formula of the prayer: 'Moses rejoiced in the gift of his portion,' etc. And He said, 'Nevertheless, you shall keep My sabbaths' — two in meaning, because He had mentioned the Tabernacle and all its utensils, and it was said (Exodus 25:8), 'And they shall make Me a sanctuary.' And above He said, 'An oil of holy anointing — this shall be to Me.' And in the Yalkut, parashat Terumah, it concludes that every place where the language 'to Me' is said, it endures forever and for all eternity — yet our eyes see that they did not endure forever, for the sanctuary is destroyed and the oil, where is it? Therefore He said, as one casting a condition upon the matter, 'Nevertheless, you shall keep My sabbaths,' and on this path our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 118b): 'If Israel were to keep two sabbaths, they would be immediately redeemed,' as it is said (Isaiah 56:4), 'who keep My sabbaths,' etc. Therefore He used the language of 'nevertheless' (אַךְ), to say: but only on this condition will the Tabernacle and the oil endure forever — if you keep two sabbaths. And it may be that this is also the opinion of Rashi, who explained that it comes to restrict the sabbath from the labor of the Tabernacle, for since the endurance of the Tabernacle depends on the keeping of the sabbath, if so, certainly the sabbath is greater than the labor of the Tabernacle; therefore it is not fitting that the labor of the Tabernacle should push aside the sabbath." "And the reason for two sabbaths specifically is this: because the sabbath has two aspects. The first is as a remembrance of the creation of the world, and this is called the sabbath of Creation. The second is the sabbath of the future, which alludes to the World to Come, the day that is entirely sabbath — and on this the commentators agreed. And it appears to me to bring proof from what is concluded in the Yalkut on this parashah: Rabbi Nathan says, from where do you say that everyone who keeps the sabbath according to its ordinance, it is accounted to him as though he kept all the sabbaths from the day the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world until the dead live, as it is said, 'And the children of Israel shall keep the sabbath,' etc.? And every discerning person will wonder at the spectacle: from where did Rabbi Nathan learn to say that there is here an allusion to the sabbath of the day the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, and to the sabbath of the resurrection of the dead? But certainly it was difficult for him: why was it said first, 'My sabbaths,' and afterward, 'the sabbath' was mentioned? And furthermore, that the whole passage speaks in direct address — 'And you shall keep the sabbath' — and afterward it is said in concealed language — 'And the children of Israel shall keep the sabbath.' And furthermore, that 'for it is a sign' is said twice." "Rather, because all the first verses until 'and they shall keep' all speak of the sabbath of Creation, and they refer to what is said, 'Nevertheless, you shall keep My sabbaths' — and afterward He explained what these two sabbaths are. And He said first concerning the sabbath of Creation: 'for it is a sign between Me and you' — He placed the sign before the statement 'between Me and you,' for the sabbath of Creation preceded the commandment to the children of Israel. And He said, 'to know that I am YHWH who sanctifies you' — for it comes as a remembrance of the creation of the world, and it is a testimony to His renewing it, and this is 'to know that I am YHWH'; and concerning it He said, 'And you shall keep the sabbath' — concerning the sabbath of Creation, until the end of the matter. And afterward, from 'And the children of Israel shall keep the sabbath,' He began with the sabbath of the future, the day that is entirely sabbath — the concealed, and 'eye has not seen,' etc. Therefore the commandment was also said in concealed language, and He said, 'to make the sabbath for their generations an eternal covenant.' In it He mentioned the language of making, for concerning the reward of the World to Come it is said (Psalms 31:20), 'How great is Your goodness that You have hidden for those who fear You; You have wrought for those who take refuge in You.' So too He said here, 'And the children of Israel shall keep the sabbath' — so that through this keeping they would bring about the making of the sabbath, that the Holy One, blessed be He, would effect and make for them the reward of the day that is entirely sabbath, 'for their generations an eternal covenant' — and this is the covenant to the World to Come. And He placed here first, 'between Me and between the children of Israel,' and afterward mentioned, 'it is a sign forever' — for the commandment preceded the thing to which the sign alludes. And from all these precise details Rabbi Nathan learned to posit these two boundaries: from the day the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:46.790369034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}